WEBVTT

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.500
And the ongoing launch in IV.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:05.870
Thank you.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:08.620
Next question.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.160
Your next question comes from the line of James Quigley from Goldman Facts.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.000
Please go ahead.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:16.840
Hello, thank you for taking my second question.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:23.660
So, you're just following up on the remoributinib food allergy data from the Phase 2 that is supporting the Phase 3 trial.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:33.420
So, what is it in the modeling that you saw that makes the 75 milligram the most appropriate dose, given the data we've seen for the 100 milligram and the 25 milligram?

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:44.680
Is there a bridge between, where the efficacy is between the two, or would the 75 milligram dose have the same target engagement as 100 milligrams?

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.060
That's a great question.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.960
So, we modeled this really carefully based on the full data set that we have.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:58.080
And we do believe that at 75 milligrams we do have full target engagement and we should be able to achieve efficacy that approaches the 100 milligram dose.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.880
But we've – and so, we felt like this was a reasonable approach to take.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:02.860
Particularly, given that we want to move into should be the article feed.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.860
We want to move into the adolescent population and FDA would surely ask us to use the minimum

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.820
required dose to achieve the efficacy required.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.880
So, based on all our modeling, we believe we can get the necessary target engagement

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.640
at 75 milligrams and then deliver the efficacy that you saw in the study, which as you could

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.300
see was, you know, was really, you know, very compelling.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.300
Next question.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.710
Thank you.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:32.090
We will now take our last question for today.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:36.430
And the last question comes from the line of Steve Scaler from TD Securities.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:37.430
Please go ahead.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.930
Oh, thank you so much for the follow-up.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:45.730
Slide 25 no longer mentions MFN, whereas the same slide in the Q4 deck did.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.370
I'm just curious why.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:53.790
I heard your earlier responses to Matthew's questions, but slide 25 suggests that Novartis

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:58.330
is increasingly confident in its ability to deal with MFN.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:02.070
And I'm just wondering what has changed as we start 2021.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.070
Slide 26.

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:05.410
Thank you.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:06.410
Hold on.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:07.410
One second, Steve.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.410
We're just trying to get slide 25 up.

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.410
It's this.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.410
Oh, okay.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:18.410
So, I think no change in our perspective on MFN.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:24.170
I mean, we've fully factored in MFN into the guidance that we've given, both for this

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.910
year and the five-year period.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.690
So, that's fully factored in.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.750
If anything, I think we've gotten even better now in modeling the MFN impact.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:32.750
So, we've made clear assumptions.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.890
We've made clear assumptions on the impact on the existing portfolio of medicines for

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:44.130
the Medicaid effect, as well as on future launches, a set of assumptions on launch phasing

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:48.130
for drugs that have a full MFN effect, and then we'll ultimately, of course, see how

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:49.130
this all unfolds.

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:53.950
We also have signed the agreements to allow us to not have an impact from tariffs with

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:58.390
the commercial agreement that we've signed with the U.S. government, as well as scaling

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:02.750
up, as you've seen, our manufacturing plants around the United States to allow us to produce

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:07.630
fully in the U.S. for the U.S., such that we wouldn't expect any tariff impact.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:11.930
So, all that to say, I guess at this point, we feel confident enough with MFN that we don't

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:13.930
need to mention it anymore.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.750
Hence, that's why you're not seeing it on the slides.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.000
Thank you.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.380
I think that is all the questions.

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:25.060
So, thank you all very, very much for an engaging discussion.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.380
I know we have some follow-ups, but we'll get back to you.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.340
We appreciate your time, as always, and we'll look forward to keeping you up-to-date in

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.340
the months ahead.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:32.680
Have a great day.

